# Making PDFs with PDFDispatch

The PDFDispatch MCP server (`https://mcp.pdfdispatch.com/mcp`) turns HTML, Markdown, web pages and Office files into
PDFs, merges PDFs and takes screenshots. Every tool that makes a file answers with a download link. Docs:
https://docs.pdfdispatch.com/mcp/

## Pick the tool

- `render_template`: a standard invoice, receipt, certificate or report. Send the data (parties, items with tax
  rates, recipient, sections), not HTML; totals and tax are computed and the design is built in. Try this first.
- `html_to_pdf`: anything with a design of its own (tickets, letters, custom invoices). Write one complete HTML page
  with the CSS in a `<style>` element.
- `markdown_to_pdf`: notes, a README or a plain report. It has a clean built-in style, so no CSS is needed.
- `url_to_pdf`: a public web page. The server cannot reach `localhost` or private addresses.
- `office_to_pdf`: Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OpenDocument, RTF and CSV files. Several files with `merge` give one PDF.
- `merge_pdfs`: two or more PDFs into one, in the order given.
- `screenshot`: HTML or a web page as an image, to check a layout before making the PDF.
- `upload_file`: sends a file from this computer to `office_to_pdf` or `merge_pdfs`.

## Write HTML that prints well

- Set the page in CSS: `@page { size: A4; margin: 15mm; }`. The server's defaults are A4 and printed backgrounds.
- Keep blocks together with `break-inside: avoid` (table rows, cards, signatures). Start a section on a new page with
  `break-before: page`.
- The server cannot read files from this computer. Put small images in the HTML as `data:` URLs, or link them from a
  public `https://` address. Use web-safe fonts, or embed the font as a `data:` URL.
- For page numbers, pass `footer_html` with elements of the classes `pageNumber` and `totalPages`; they are filled in
  on every page.
- For PDF/A, a password or other API options, pass them in `extra_fields` by their API name, for example
  `{"pdfa": "PDF/A-2b"}`.

## Check the layout when it matters

You cannot see a PDF, but you can see an image. For a layout that must be right (a certificate, a one-page invoice),
call `screenshot` with the same HTML first, look at it, fix what is off, then make the PDF. Each screenshot counts as
one PDF on the user's plan, so skip it for simple documents.

## Save the result at once

- Result links work for one hour. Download the file right away:
  `curl -fsSL -o out/invoice-1042.pdf "<link>"`.
- Save into the folder the user names. If they name none, use `out/` in the project and create it if needed.
- Do not paste result links into issues, commits or chats: anyone with a link can download the file during its hour.

## Files on this computer

For `office_to_pdf` and `merge_pdfs`, a local file needs a link first:

1. Call `upload_file` with the file name, extension included (`contract.docx`).
2. Upload it: `curl -fsS -T contract.docx "<upload_url>"` (the upload link works for 15 minutes).
3. Pass `file_url` to the next tool. Results of earlier calls are links already and can go in directly.

## Cost and errors

- Each call that makes a file counts as one PDF on the user's plan. Uploads are free, and so are calls that fail.
- `401`: the user is not signed in, the sign-in ran out, or the connection was revoked on the Keys page. They sign in
  again (Claude Code: `/mcp`, pick the server, Authenticate). With a key in the client's MCP config instead, check that
  it reaches the server.
- Other errors name the API's code and link to its explanation, for example code `42910` (too many requests in a
  minute: wait and try again). All codes: https://docs.pdfdispatch.com/limits/#errors
